# The installer payload: built binaries staged for chaos-setup to embed.
# Regenerated by the release workflow; never committed.
stage/

# Gradle's build output for the Android client. Everything here is generated
# from `android/app/src`, and an APK plus its intermediates is tens of
# megabytes of churn per build.
android/.gradle/
android/build/
android/app/build/
android/local.properties

# **The JNI libraries are build output, not source.** `android/app/src/main/
# jniLibs/**/libchaos_android.so` is 490 KB per ABI produced by cross-compiling
# `android/jni`, and committing it would put a binary in the tree that nobody
# can review and that goes stale the moment the Rust changes. Build it with
# `scripts/build-android-jni.ps1`; the app runs without it as a client.
android/app/src/main/jniLibs/

# Same reasoning, for the brand pages. `chaos-serve --emit-pages` writes the
# mark and the reader into the APK's assets, wrapped in a document with the
# fonts embedded -- about 600 KB of generated HTML whose source is
# `assets/grimoire/`. Committing it would be committing a build product that
# goes stale silently. Release CI regenerates it; a local `gradlew assemble`
# without it still works, and BrandActivity falls back to the node.
android/app/src/main/assets/brand/

# The quality gate's baselines. **Per-machine and per-build, deliberately not
# shared.** A baseline is what *this* engine did before *this* change, so it is
# recorded minutes before it is compared against; a committed one would be
# somebody else's build on somebody else's SIMD path, and the first difference
# would be read as a regression. `scripts/quality-gate.sh` records one on
# demand and says so.
quality-baseline/

# **A signing key must never be committed.** The repository is public, and a
# committed key lets anyone build an APK Android accepts as an upgrade over a
# user's install. `scripts/make-release-keystore.sh` writes both of these.
*.keystore
*.keystore.b64
*.jks
